{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/18693-title-question-why-wraith-and-changeling-instead-of-ghost-and-faerie","id":18693,"title":"Title question: Why \"Wraith\" and \"Changeling\" instead of \"Ghost\" and \"Faerie?\"","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2013-11-26T13:54:20-08:00","id":18693,"title":"Title question: Why \"Wraith\" and \"Changeling\" instead of \"Ghost\" and \"Faerie?\"","content":"I'm gearing up to start a new <b>Wraith: the Oblivion</b> chronicle. (Yay!) Going through old sourcebooks has brought up an old question that I've never found an answer for.<br /> <br /> In older sourcebooks, references to the game lines in the classic World of Darkness referred to <b>Vampire</b>, <b>Werewolf</b>, <b>Mage</b>, <b>Faerie</b>, and <b>Ghost</b>. That was in times when only Vampire and Werewolf were published, circa 1992 or so. Fast-forward to late 1995, and we have <b>Vampire</b>, <b>Werewolf</b>, <b>Mage</b>, <b>Wraith</b>, and <b>Changeling</b>.<br /> <br /> Why the change in titles?<br /> <br /> I've heard a variety of theories ranging from the armchair-lawyer stuff to the utterly bizarre. While I'm glad for any insights, a citation would be lovely.<br /> <br /> Thanks!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/902-dykstrav","name":"Dykstrav","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-26T14:13:23-08:00","id":18704,"title":"","content":"My guess would be that someone thought Wraith and Changeling sounded cooler than Ghost and Faerie.<br /> <br /> Also, the main stream pop culture view of what a faerie is has changed significantly since then. At the time, there wasn't much faerie related beyond Tinkerbell. Now, we have stuff like Lost Girl, Grimm, and Once Upon a Time on TV and Laurell K. Hamilton's Merry Gentry books plus Jim Butcher's Dresden Files.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Charlie Cantrell</b><br /> Onyx Path Freelancer <hr />Changeling: The Dreaming 20th Anniversary Edition, Conquering Heroes, Book of Freeholds, Guide to the Night, C20 Players Guide","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/648-pookaknight","name":"PookaKnight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar648_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"690"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-26T14:20:35-08:00","id":18712,"title":"","content":"Changeling also hits up a slightly different mythological context as opposed to just using another word for the same thing. Changelings are fae creatures left with human parents instead of the fair folk living in mysterious places out in the wilds. Since CtD is about human-fae hybrids, the name makes more sense. This even holds over to Dark Ages: Fae, which has other types of fae besides changelings.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-26T19:12:50-08:00","id":18879,"title":"","content":"Well, &quot;Wraith&quot; has much darker connotations than &quot;Ghost&quot; does, and &quot;Fairies&quot; certainly did relate more to flitty-flying pixies than it did the more fantastic-origins-of-dreams.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">- Arthur &quot;Torakhan&quot; Dreese <span style=\"color:silver\"><b>David &quot;Wall-of-Text&quot; Tealdeer</b> - US2012070036\u200b - Grand Rapids, MI</span><br /> <img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/a/img855/1250/jv8k.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><br /> <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/nmeqe3m\" target=\"_blank\">V:tM 20th</a> \u2022 <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/pavtnjj\" target=\"_blank\">Hunters Hunted II</a> \u2022 <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/l3hjgjt\" target=\"_blank\">Anarchs Unbound</a> \u2022 <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/mxyvsy9\" target=\"_blank\">W:tA 20th</a> \u2022 <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/k5qe4j4\" target=\"_blank\">W20 Changing Breeds</a> \u2022 <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/m64m9o5\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted 3rd Edition</a> \u2022 <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/qxkop7m\" target=\"_blank\">M:tA 20th</a> \u2022 <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/nd6kms2\" target=\"_blank\">W20BotW</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/780-torakhan","name":"Torakhan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar780_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"576"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-26T19:25:00-08:00","id":18884,"title":"","content":"I'm not sure Wraith really has a &quot;darker&quot; connotation. It's just another word for ghost. Where does it get used to mean something darker than ghost? Esp. significantly before WtO? The &quot;dark&quot; use seems to stem completely from RPGs (notably D&amp;D) reinterpreting LotR. Wraith is a word the Tolkien used because he was a linguist and used archaic terms to help create atmosphere.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-27T04:39:16-08:00","id":19090,"title":"","content":"I believe the word 'Ghost' brings to mind different streotypes of the era than it does now. Ghostbusters anyone? You hear the word Ghost and think Patrick Swayze and spooky haunted houses. Wraith as a game setting barely touches on those aspects that people would automatically associate with the game. the term &quot;Wraith&quot; on the other hand is used for more ominous manifestations. The Lord of the Rings was still very popular even though there were no Peter Jackson movies yet and anyone who's a fan of those books will know that the Ring Wraiths were the scariest damn things out there aside from Sauron himself. So if I had to name a game Ghost or Wraith and convey the utmost scariness possible, I'd go with the word Wraith too.<br /> <br /> As for Changeling I think Heavy Arms and PookaKnight has hit the nail on the head. It just fits better.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/19/w20supporterheavymetal1.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/31/25ew.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/829/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><a href=\"http://glyphery.webs.com/\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://i.imgur.com/Ut0SZ4h.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1332-darling_rose","name":"Darling_Rose","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1332_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"434"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-27T07:28:52-08:00","id":19165,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/18693-title-question-why-wraith-and-changeling-instead-of-ghost-and-faerie?p=18712#post18712\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This even holds over to Dark Ages: Fae, which has other types of fae besides changelings.</div> </div> </div> </div>While you're right, it's not the only reason for the name change. If I recall correctly from BlackHat Matt's comments at the time, they wanted to make sure folks saw it as something distinct and different from Changeling: The Dreaming and not just a historical supplement for a poorly selling game line.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Charlie Cantrell</b><br /> Onyx Path Freelancer <hr />Changeling: The Dreaming 20th Anniversary Edition, Conquering Heroes, Book of Freeholds, Guide to the Night, C20 Players Guide","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/648-pookaknight","name":"PookaKnight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar648_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"690"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-27T08:02:21-08:00","id":19183,"title":"","content":"I'm neutral to the word &quot;wraith&quot; itself, but in my experience, it's not as clearly and widely understood as &quot;ghost.&quot; When the product line was named, someone had a good reason for choosing &quot;wraith&quot; instead of &quot;ghost,&quot; that's what I'm looking for.<br /> <br /> Last night I checked out the 1st edition Storyteller's Handbook for <b>Vampire</b>, where they actually describe a generic faerie and ghost in the antagonists section (and yes, they were called faeries and ghosts, not changelings and wraiths). The ghost described there is pretty close to the characters we get in <b>Wraith</b>. The faerie seems a bit different than what we get in <b>Changeling</b>, however. They are described as literal faeries that travel back and forth from their extraplanar realm, not as faerie souls trapped in human bodies. They also mention how faeries often kidnap human children, although they don't outright state that they replace kidnapped children with faeries.<br /> <br /> It seems that even back in 1992 or so, the designers had some ideas about what those future games would look like... Then those plans changed. That's what I'm getting at. That's what I'm curious about.<br /> <br /> About the specific titles... When I introduce the game to new people and tell them that it's <b>Wraith: the Oblivion</b>, the reaction is often one of confusion. It's often along the lines of, &quot;What's a wraith? Like the Nazg\u00fbl out of Tolkien?&quot; To which I'll reply, &quot;You're playing a ghost. That's just a Scottish word for 'ghost' that the publisher decided on for the title of this game line.&quot; As soon as I tell people that they're playing ghosts, they get it. In the same way, you don't have to explain <b>Vampire</b> or <b>Werewolf</b> to new players unfamiliar with the game--everyone knows what a vampire or werewolf is. Yet the reaction might be different if the publisher had decided to name those game lines, say, <b>Vrykolaka</b> or <b>Fear-Faol</b>.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/902-dykstrav","name":"Dykstrav","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-27T08:14:52-08:00","id":19187,"title":"","content":"Well, aren't Wraiths and their corresponding Underworld more like the Nazgul than Ghostbusters?<br /> <br /> Not really that far off is it?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>- If you must be ridiculous, I must ridicule you.<br /> - Those that can give up essential liberties in exchange for temporary safety deserve neither liberty nor safety. -- Benjamin Franklin</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/375-papa-bear","name":"Papa Bear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar375_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1920"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-27T08:22:52-08:00","id":19191,"title":"","content":"I'm sure the change from Ghost to Wraith was purely a stylistic choice. Wraith sounds more goth than Ghost, and most folks know Wraith is a synonym for Ghost.<br /> <br /> As for Changeling's changes, there were five years between when Vampire was first published and when Changeling was first published. That's more than enough time to go from a vague idea of &quot;hey, let's do a faerie game!&quot; to develop and refine ideas (and get heavily inspired by Neil Gaiman's Sandman <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> ) into something quite different from what was in the back of Vampire 1st edition. Not to mention that in those five years White Wolf went from a handful of folks operating out of a garage to a full blown publishing company with quite a few full time staffers. There were a lot more voices pitching ideas when it was time to actually sit down and write Changeling than there were when Vampire was published.<br /> <br /> I do remember after Dark Ages: Fae came out, BlackHat Matt said that while he was developing it, he intentionally tried to make the fae in it closer to the old 1st edition Vampire version of faeries. I thought it was a neat idea to have the fae in the World of Darkness change over time in the same way they were written about in the game books.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Charlie Cantrell</b><br /> Onyx Path Freelancer <hr />Changeling: The Dreaming 20th Anniversary Edition, Conquering Heroes, Book of Freeholds, Guide to the Night, C20 Players Guide","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/648-pookaknight","name":"PookaKnight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar648_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"690"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-28T03:39:48-08:00","id":19642,"title":"","content":"It would be nice to see some of that creep back into a Changeling 20 edition. Something that would give every fae and inevitable taste of Unseelieness.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/19/w20supporterheavymetal1.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/31/25ew.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/829/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><a href=\"http://glyphery.webs.com/\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://i.imgur.com/Ut0SZ4h.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1332-darling_rose","name":"Darling_Rose","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1332_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"434"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-29T09:52:16-08:00","id":20227,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PookaKnight</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/18693-title-question-why-wraith-and-changeling-instead-of-ghost-and-faerie?p=19191#post19191\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm sure the change from Ghost to Wraith was purely a stylistic choice. Wraith sounds more goth than Ghost, and most folks know Wraith is a synonym for Ghost.</div> </div> </div> </div>This... Which is pretty okay. I mean, I DO have a couple of older <b>Vampire: the Masquerade</b> books that promise 'Ghost' and 'Fairy' games (maybe not exactly, but that's what I remember, here at work, far from my books), and those names didn't really resonate with me.<br /> <br /> As for knowing it was a synonym? Before I got <b>Wraith: the Oblivion</b> Second Edition, I kinda thought that a wraith was a special kind of ghost in the games. I'm no dummy, but I didn't know for sure how the game world worked. After reading the book, I was a little surprised how rarely the term 'wraith' actually came up. At least in <b>Changeling: the Dreaming</b> they actually call themselves changelings! Regardless, looking forward to a chance to support both getting twentieth anniversary editions!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>B.I.G. Bird spreads the word: Anybody with a heart votes love<br /> <a href=\"https://absintheandthorns.obsidianportal.com/\" target=\"_blank\">https://absintheandthorns.obsidianportal.com/</a></i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/409-phaolan","name":"Phaolan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar409_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"478"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-30T15:38:21-08:00","id":20826,"title":"","content":"I figured the Ghost/Wraith thing was two fold.<br /> <br /> First, that CWoD had rules for Ghosts prior to W:tO, and those rules were for a class of ephemeral being totally different from Wraiths. Ghosts haunt the living world, they use powers different from Wraiths, etc., et al. <br /> <br /> Second, The specific choice of the term Wraith probably does link back into Tolkien and the Barrow-Wraiths, and thence D&amp;D. Wraiths get classes as a different form of spirit, a DARKER form of spirit, than Ghosts, and all this goes into justifying having them be different creatures than Ghosts.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/509-tiwaztyrsfist","name":"TiwazTyrsfist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar509_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"162"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-04T15:24:22-08:00","id":23344,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/18693-title-question-why-wraith-and-changeling-instead-of-ghost-and-faerie?p=18884#post18884\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm not sure Wraith really has a &quot;darker&quot; connotation. It's just another word for ghost. Where does it get used to mean something darker than ghost? Esp. significantly before WtO? The &quot;dark&quot; use seems to stem completely from RPGs (notably D&amp;D) reinterpreting LotR. Wraith is a word the Tolkien used because he was a linguist and used archaic terms to help create atmosphere.</div> </div> </div> </div>A ghost is someone in a sheet that says &quot;boo!&quot;<br /> A wraith is an otherworldly being from beyond death.<br /> <br /> Sorta like why it's &quot;Mage&quot; (a scholar who dabbles in the forces of the universe&quot; and not &quot;Wizard&quot; (guy in a pointy hat casting magic missile at the darkness)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/612-the-cat-came-back","name":"The Cat Came Back","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar612_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"243"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-04T23:55:39-08:00","id":23576,"title":"","content":"&quot;Wraith&quot; meaning &quot;an otherworldly being from beyond death&quot; is, essentially, an invention of D&amp;D. Before that it was no more loaded a word than ghost or specter. If you look at the Middle Earth stuff, wraith gets applied to both your run of the mill dead people spirits still hanging around for some reason and otherworldly beings from beyond death (though they are still just magically powerful dead people, they're not something else). Before Tolkien it is a pretty obscure word that doesn't seem to have any such connotations.<br /> <br /> And mage doesn't mean &quot;a scholar who dabbles in the forces of the universe&quot; it means a person that does magic. Mage and wizard are synonyms. They mean the same thing. MtA was probably called Mage because D&amp;D (again drastically reinterpreting from Tolkien) got to wizard first. I doubt there was any larger choice beyond, &quot;we don't want people to think this is D&amp;D style wizards,&quot; beyond wanting people to associate Mage with Ars Magica (where wizard and magus are used pretty much interchangeably).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}